Here's a quick start for building NetHack with MinGW as of December 20,  
2004:

( [] contains commands you type in at the command prompt
   {mingwdir} represents the location where you chose to install
              MinGW.
   {gnudir}   represents the location where you chose to install
              the gnuwin32 utilities below.)

  1. Obtain gzip if you don't already have it on your system.
     To get gzip, download:
     http://prdownloads.sf.net/gnuwin32/gzip-1.3.5-bin.exe?download
     Execute gzip-1.3.5-bin.exe to install gzip.exe, and more importantly
     for our purposes here, gunzip.exe. Whatever directory you chose to
     install it to, we'll be referencing as {gnudir} in instructions
     further down.
  2. Obtain tar if you don't already have it on your system.
     To get tar, download:
     http://prdownloads.sf.net/gnuwin32/tar-1.13-1-bin.exe?download
     Execute tar-1.13-1-bin.exe to install tar.exe. Put it in the
     same directory as you put gzip.
  3. Obtain and install latest full MinGW base kit from:
     http://prdownloads.sf.net/mingw/mingw-3.1.0-1.exe?download
     Install the MinGW base kit by running mingw-3.1.0-1.exe. Whatever
     directory you chose to install it to we'll be referencing as {mingwdir}
     in later steps below.
  4. Obtain the 3.4.2 gcc compiler update for MinGW from:
     http://prdownloads.sf.net/mingw/gcc-core-3.4.2-20040916-1.tar.gz?download
     Save the file to the top of the MinGW installation tree, which we'll
     refer to as {mingwdir}, the one with the bin, doc, include, lib, mingw
     subdirectories in it.
  5. Obtain the MinGW 3.5 runtime update from:
     http://prdownloads.sf.net/mingw/mingw-runtime-3.5.tar.gz?download
     and save it to {mingwdir}.
  6. Obtain the MinGW Windows API update from:
     http://prdownloads.sf.net/mingw/w32api-3.1.tar.gz?download
     and save it to {mingwdir}.
  7. Unpack the MinGW updates from the top of the {mingw} tree,
     the one with the bin, doc, include, lib, mingw subdirectories in it.
     [cd {mingwdir}]
     [{gnudir}\bin\gunzip gcc-core-3.4.2-20040916-1.tar.gz]
     [{gnudir}\bin\gunzip mingw-runtime-3.5.tar.gz]
     [{gnudir}\bin\gunzip w32api-3.1.tar.gz]
     [{gnudir}\bin\tar -xvf gcc-core-3.4.2-20040916-1.tar]
     [{gnudir}\bin\tar -xvf mingw-runtime-3.5.tar]
     [{gnudir}\bin\tar -xvf w32api-3.1.tar]
  8. Download the current NetHack sources.
     http://prdownloads.sf.net/nethack/nethack-343-src.tgz?download
  9. Unpack the NetHack sources.
     [{gnudir}\bin\gunzip nethack-343-src.tgz]
     [{gnudir}\bin\tar -xvf nethack-343-src.tar]
10. Go to the top of the NetHack source tree you just unpacked.
11. Navigate into the sys\winnt directory
     [cd sys\winnt]
12. Run the bat file that will move the Makefiles for you, and
     ensure that things are ready to go.
     [nhsetup.bat]
13. Navigate from the NetHack sys\winnt directory to the NetHack
     src directory.
     [cd ..\..\src]
14. Decide whether you want to build the tty version (NetHack.exe)
     or the gui version (NetHackW.exe). For the former (tty version),
     do nothing in this step, that is what the Makefile builds out
     of the box.
     For the gui version, use a text editor to edit Makefile.gcc and
     change the line:
             #GRAPHICAL = Y
     to
             GRAPHICAL = Y
     In other words, "uncomment that line."
15. [mingw32-make -f Makefile.gcc install]
     ...much activity...
16. [cd ..\binary]
17. [nethack]
18. "Do you want your possessions identified?" :-)

*Additional note from Karl Garrison [paraphrased slightly]:
     If you also have cygwin installed on your system, you may have
     to set PATH so that only MinGW is in it; (e.g. path=c:\MinGW\bin).
     Failure to do so prior to step 9 may lead to a bunch of Makefile
     syntax errors, even if MinGW is in your PATH before cygwin, and
     you are using the correct make.

*Additional note to avoid a warning when compiling util/panic.c:
     This block of code in include/ntconf.h:
	#ifndef alloca
	#define ALLOCA_HACK	/* used in util/panic.c */
	#endif
     may have to be changed to:
	#ifndef alloca
		/* bison generated foo_yacc.c might try to use alloca() */
	# ifdef __GNUC__
	#  define alloca __builtin_alloca
	# else
	#  define ALLOCA_HACK	/* used in util/panic.c */
	# endif
	#endif

